INSERT INTO canonical_records (record_id,source_file,source_page,domain,stem,options_json,source_answer,source_explanation,provenance_json,visual_asset,visual_url,options_mode,current_answer,current_answer_status,current_evidence_json,currentness_risk,record_json,active_quiz,import_version,imported_at) VALUES ('a0375838635f6fbcad6b','AMEDEX 2025 Psychiatry  (3).pdf','96','Mental Health','(Options A, B and D) While an MMSE score of
<25 is likely in both dementia and
pseudodementia, a score of >25 almost excludes
dementia. Alzheimer''s disease, Pick disease and
multi-infarct dementia are often associated with a
marked decrease in MMSE score.
(Option C) Although tiredness can be seen in
some patients with anxiety disorders, particularly in
those with generalised anxiety disorders,
forgetfulness is not a common feature.
NOTE - The history of disturbances in
pseudodementia is often short and of abrupt
onset, whereas dementia is often more
insidious. Clinically, people with
pseudodementia differ from those with true
dementia when their memory is tested. They
typically answer that they do not know the
answer to a question, and their attention and
concentration are often intact, and they may
appear upset or distressed when they fail to
answer. Those with true dementia will give
wrong answers, have poor attention and
concentration, and seem indifferent or
unconcerned.
Reference(s) °« Medscape - What is pseudodeme
